## 2. Root cause (one cause, two manifestations)

BOTH reds are the LSP MCP row's declared dependency being unresolvable from the tree the row's launcher
lives in:

* the row (`cordis.patch.yml` `- id: mcp-lsp`) runs `packages/mpd-mcp-lsp/dist/launch.js`, which resolves
  the npm package `cclsp` from the `node_modules` chain ABOVE ITS OWN FILE
  (`packages/mpd-mcp-shared/dependency-entry.ts`; the launcher's own header states tier 3 is a manual
  `node_modules` walk from the launcher's directory);
* without it the launcher takes its `serveUnavailable` branch
  (`packages/mpd-mcp-shared/unavailable-server.ts`): the row stays ALIVE and answers `tools/list` with an
  EMPTY list — "this row exposes NO tools until the dependency loads";
* every failing clause is an LSP clause, and nothing else differs:
  * `mcp-call`'s enum arm requires an `mcp__lsp__*` name (`hasLsp`), and its call arm was GREEN;
  * `readonly-deny`'s canonical eight names include `mcp__lsp__rename_symbol` and
    `mcp__lsp__rename_symbol_strict`, so the parent can only show 6 of 8, the roles row's tolerant
    prune drops the two unregistered ones from the list it sends, and `denyListMatchesTheEight` is false.
    (`pruneRecordSeen=false` is NOT evidence against that: `findPruneRecord()` matches the CONTROL lane's
    verbatim line, which names `str_replace_editor, apply_patch`, not an LSP prune.)
* WHY the oneclick container has no `node_modules` above the launcher, while the source lane does:
  `docker/entrypoint.sh` SKIPS `bun install` (step 07) and the dist rebuild (step 08) in oneclick mode
  (lines 764-782: "this lane's whole claim is that a user's install runs NO build"), and the owed cases
  are run from that same checkout copy — `run_step 16c-owed-cases … --repo "$APP_DIR"` (line 2182-2183),
  i.e. `/opt/mpd`, in BOTH modes. The container's oneclick failure is therefore the apparatus's
  un-materialized dependency closure, not a statement about the published package: the PUBLISHED install
  DOES carry `cclsp` (root `package.json` `optionalDependencies`) and its own boot registered the row —
  `boot.mcpToolNaming=true … registered: ast_grep,codegraph,lsp`, and `restore.lspBootstrap=true` — both
  read from the SAME oneclick run.

The entrypoint already learned this lesson once, for the LSP arm:
`LSP_TREE="$APP_DIR"; if [ -f "$PROFILE_DIR/node_modules/@mpd-dsh/mpd/packages/mpd-mcp-lsp/dist/launch.js" ]; then LSP_TREE="$PROFILE_DIR/…"` — "the INSTALLED tree is preferred whenever the profile carries one:
in source mode it is the same checkout through the `link:`, and in one-click mode it is the only tree
that has its dependencies" (lines 2115-2125). The two QA cases were never given the equivalent.

One extra measurement explains why the SOURCE lane passed `mcp-call`: the case stages the release pack
with `npm install file:<pack>`, and **npm installs a directory `file:` dependency as a SYMLINK**
(`raw/npm-file-dependency-is-a-symlink.txt`: `node_modules/fake-pkg -> ../../fake-pkg`), while node
resolves a main module's REALPATH — so the pack's launcher resolved `cclsp` out of the CHECKOUT's own
`node_modules`, i.e. out of `bun install`, not out of the tree the case had staged. That is the same
"self-manufactured pass" the case's own header records for the ast-grep binary.

## 3. Class per case

* `qa.mcpCall` — **(b) a case/probe expectation that does not hold for the tree the case boots**: its
  enum arm asserts a NAME LIST member (`mcp__lsp__*`) that the staged pack cannot supply (the pack's own
  manifest does not declare `cclsp`, and nothing else in the tree under test does), and it passed in the
  source lane only by inheriting the checkout's closure through npm's `file:` symlink. NOT a defect of
  the published tree: the root manifest declares `cclsp@0.7.0` and the oneclick container's own install
  registered the `lsp` row.
* `qa.readonlyDeny` — **(b) same class**: the positive lane requires two of the canonical eight names to
  be REGISTERED (its anti-vacuity guard for "the child lacks them"), which the case's own
  `scripts/install-profile.ts` profile can only satisfy if the checkout carries the LSP dependency's
  closure; the oneclick lane's checkout never does. The case had not declared or materialized that
  prerequisite — its self-test even tolerates `cclsp` being absent from the checkout.
* Neither is flakiness (c): the failure is deterministic in the oneclick tree and was reproduced
  locally with byte-identical numbers (§5), and it is not model-dependent (both cases' non-LSP arms pass
  in the same run).

Separate finding, class (a), NOT the cause of these two reds (reported, not edited — see §7):
the PACKED artifact's manifest drops three entries the published root manifest declares, `cclsp` among
them, so an install that materializes the pack's declared dependency closure cannot register the LSP
tool surface. `raw/pack-vs-root-manifest.txt`.

## 4. Repair (case layer — `skills/dsh-qa/**`, the legitimate writer for class (b))

New shared helper `skills/dsh-qa/scripts/lib/mcp-dependency.ts`: reads the version the PUBLISHED root
manifest declares for an optional dependency and materializes it into a given tree, offline first —
(1) already present, (2) copy from an ISOLATED checkout store (`node_modules/.bun/<pkg>@<ver>/node_modules`,
2.2 MB / 7 entries for `cclsp@0.7.0`), (3) `npm install <name>@<version> --prefix <tree> --cache
<tree>/.npm-cache`.

* `skills/dsh-qa/scripts/mcp-call.ts`: the case now boots a SANDBOX COPY of the release pack (so the
  launcher's realpath is sandbox-local and the checkout's closure can no longer leak in) and stages the
  declared `cclsp` into that copy before the profile install; the staging tier is recorded in
  `result.json` and on the printed verdict line, and a new self-test arm asserts the manifest still
  declares the dependency and that the live arm still stages it.
* `skills/dsh-qa/scripts/readonly-deny.ts`: each lane copies the SHIPPED launcher bytes into the sandbox
  (`<dshHome>/lsp-mcp/launch.js`) with the declared dependency staged beside them, and repoints ONLY the
  sandbox's `mcp-lsp` row at that copy; the ledger gains `mcpLspDependency` (tier + tree per lane), and
  two self-test arms pin the declaration and the staging/repoint calls.
* Assertion strength is unchanged: the eight-name list, `parentSeesAllDenyNames`, the child-restriction
  assertion and the re-injection control all still have to hold, and the control lane still proves the
  tolerant prune path on real data. Nothing was removed to obtain a pass.

## 5. Local reproduction and re-run (no Docker)

`/tmp` and `/dev/shm` are fresh per bash call (T-23) and bwrap cannot create namespaces here, so the
oneclick condition was reproduced with two devices, both under the gitignored scratch root
`.qa-oneclick/`: (a) `sim-src` / `sim-oc` — hardlink clones of the checkout, `sim-oc` without
`node_modules`; (b) a manifest-only `cclsp` at the first ancestor `node_modules` whose entry EXISTs but
serves zero tools (`raw/local-*`, `raw/local-run-matrix.json`), i.e. the launcher's own
`serveUnavailable` state; (c) `TMPDIR` pointed into the scratch root so the case sandboxes survive the
call.

PRE-FIX, oneclick condition — the container's signature reproduced locally, byte for byte:

    [mcp-call] ok=false (enum recorded tools=95, ast_grep call recorded) -> …
      enum.hasLsp=false  enum.hasAstGrep=true  call.called=true  call.succeeded=true  call.resultHasFixtureLine=true
    [readonly-deny] toolCount=95  writeCapableVisible=6 (no mcp__lsp__*)  parentSeesAllDenyNames=false
                    denyListMatchesTheEight=false  pruneRecordSeen=false  exit=1

PRE-FIX, source condition (same clone WITH `node_modules`): 107 tools, all eight write-capable names,
`denyListMatchesTheEight=true`, PASS. The name-level diff between the two conditions is EXACTLY the
cclsp family and nothing else (12 names):
`mcp__lsp__{find_definition,find_implementation,find_references,find_workspace_symbols,get_diagnostics,get_hover,get_incoming_calls,get_outgoing_calls,prepare_call_hierarchy,rename_symbol,rename_symbol_strict,restart_server}`.

POST-FIX (both cases, both conditions):

| run | tree | command | observed |
|---|---|---|---|
| oneclick condition | `.qa-oneclick/sim-oc` (+ the zero-tool `cclsp` ancestor) | `node skills/dsh-qa/scripts/mcp-call.ts` | `ok=true (enum recorded tools=107, mcp__ast_grep__=true, mcp__lsp__=true, ast_grep call recorded, call result has the fixture line=true, lsp dependency staged from npm-install)`, `[mcp-call] PASS`, exit 0 |
| oneclick condition | same | `node skills/dsh-qa/scripts/readonly-deny.ts` | `ok=true`, `parentSeesAllDenyNames=true`, `denyListMatchesTheEight=true`, `mcpLspDependency.ok=true source=npm-install`, `[readonly-deny] PASS`, exit 0 |
| checkout (source) | the repository | `node skills/dsh-qa/scripts/mcp-call.ts` | `ok=true (… lsp dependency staged from checkout-store)`, `[mcp-call] PASS`, exit 0 |
| checkout (source) | the repository | `node skills/dsh-qa/scripts/readonly-deny.ts` | `ok=true`, eight names intact, `mcpLspDependency.ok=true source=checkout-store`, `[readonly-deny] PASS`, exit 0 |

Both tiers are therefore exercised: the oneclick condition takes the npm tier (its route inside the
container), a normally installed checkout takes the offline store-copy tier.

