## 7. Finding for the captain — class (a), NOT edited here

`scripts/pack-mpd.ts` writes the packed manifest's `optionalDependencies` as a literal
(`raw/pack-vs-root-manifest.txt`):

    root package.json  optionalDependencies = {"@colbymchenry/codegraph":"1.5.0","@cyanheads/git-mcp-server":"2.15.3","cclsp":"0.7.0","mcp-server-commands":"0.8.2"}
    pack package.json  optionalDependencies = {"@ast-grep/cli":"0.45.2","@colbymchenry/codegraph":"1.5.0","@code-yeongyu/comment-checker":"0.8.0"}
    declared by the ROOT but ABSENT from the pack: ["@cyanheads/git-mcp-server","cclsp","mcp-server-commands"]

`cclsp` is the LIVE `mcp-lsp` row's declared dependency (the `mcp-git`/`mcp-shell` rows that need the
other two are `disabled: true`), so an install that materializes the pack's declared closure — the
tarball path documented in AGENTS §8 — cannot register the 12-name LSP tool surface. Bounded proposal:
(1) derive the packed `optionalDependencies` from the root manifest (union with the two toolchain entries
the pack already adds) in `scripts/pack-mpd.ts`; (2) add a gate arm (`verify-pack-closure` or
`verify-plugin-manifest --pack`) asserting every npm package a shipped row's launcher resolves is
declared by the packed manifest; (3) re-run the pack and Docker lanes. Not applied here: `packages/**`,
the manifest and the packer are outside this seat's write scope for this wave. Note this omission is NOT
what reddened the two cases (the pack is staged by `npm file:`, which symlinks and consults no manifest
closure) — it is an independent defect found while proving that.

